## 🔬 testsmells.org 19-Smell Academic Audit — `OrderProcessorTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest 4.1.0 · .NET 10 · 9 test methods · 1 test class

---

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 4           | 4              |
| Low      | 1           | 8 (fixture-wide)|
| **Total**| **9**       | **8 of 9 methods** |

Only **1 test** (`ValidateOrder_NullOrder_ThrowsArgumentNullException`) is smell-free.

---

### High Severity

#### 1. Conditional Test Logic  
**Affected:** `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 24)  
One branch is never tested — the test adapts to whatever it gets, so it can never fail for the wrong reason.

```csharp
// ❌ SMELL — if/else gates which assertion runs
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

**Fix:** Write two deterministic tests with controlled inputs:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_Above100_SetsPremiumStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-EXPENSIVE", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_AtOrBelow100_SetsStandardStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-CHEAP", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One branch is permanently untested; a regression in that path will never be caught.

---

#### 2. Assertion-Free Test (Unknown Test)  
**Affected:** `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43)  
No assertion at all — the test passes as long as no exception is thrown, giving false confidence.

```csharp
// ❌ SMELL — exercises code but asserts nothing
processor.ProcessOrder(order);
// no Assert.*
```

**Fix:** Assert on the expected outcome:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
    Assert.IsTrue(result.TotalAmount > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** `ProcessOrder` could return garbage or silently corrupt state — the test would still pass.

---

#### 3. Sleepy Test  
**Affected:** `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` (line 91)  
`Thread.Sleep(2000)` introduces non-determinism and slows the suite.

```csharp
// ❌ SMELL — hard-coded 2-second sleep
processor.ProcessOrderAsync(order);
Thread.Sleep(2000);
Assert.IsTrue(_email.WasNotificationSent(order.Id));
```

**Fix:** Use a polling/wait helper or make the async operation awaitable:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    await processor.ProcessOrderAsync(order);

    Assert.IsTrue(_email.WasNotificationSent(order.Id));
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** Flaky failures in CI (2 s may not be enough under load); inflates suite runtime.

